 W(2, PACK, '`a, b, c = "x", "y", "z"`. In one line, rotate them so `a` gets `b`\'s value, `b` gets `c`\'s and `c` gets `a`\'s. Print all three.', 'a, b, c = "x", "y", "z"\n', 'a, b, c = "x", "y", "z"\na, b, c = b, c, a\nprint(a, b, c)', "Python packs the right side into a tuple first, then unpacks it, so no value is lost."),
 F(2, METH, "This code should print `1`, the position of `\"ink\"`, but it crashes. Fix it.", 'items = ("pen", "ink")\nprint(items.find("ink"))', 'items = ("pen", "ink")\nprint(items.index("ink"))', "`find()` is a string method. Tuples only have `count()` and `index()`."),
 # ---------- Challenge (same chapter, combined) ----------
 W(3, METH, '`temps = (31, 33, 31, 35, 31, 33)`. Print each different temperature once, in order of first appearance, with how many times it appears: `31: 3`, `33: 2`, `35: 1`. Use `count()` and `index()`.', 'temps = (31, 33, 31, 35, 31, 33)\n', 'temps = (31, 33, 31, 35, 31, 33)\nfor pos, t in enumerate(temps):\n    if temps.index(t) == pos:\n        print(f"{t}: {temps.count(t)}")', "`index(t)` gives the first position of `t`. If it equals the current position, this is the first time we see `t`."),
 W(3, IDX, '`records = (("Asha", 82), ("Ravi", 67), ("Neha", 91))`. Print `Average: 80.0`, then `Above: Asha Neha` (the names above the average).', 'records = (("Asha", 82), ("Ravi", 67), ("Neha", 91))\n', 'records = (("Asha", 82), ("Ravi", 67), ("Neha", 91))\ntotal = 0\nfor name, marks in records:\n    total += marks\naverage = total / len(records)\nabove = []\nfor name, marks in records:\n    if marks > average:\n        above.append(name)\nprint("Average:", average)\nprint("Above:", " ".join(above))', "Two passes: the first works out the average, the second compares each mark with it."),
 W(3, PACK, 'Each sale is `(product, qty, price)`: `sales = (("Tea", 3, 20), ("Coffee", 2, 45), ("Juice", 1, 60))`. Unpack each sale in the `for` line and print the total bill.', 'sales = (("Tea", 3, 20), ("Coffee", 2, 45), ("Juice", 1, 60))\n', 'sales = (("Tea", 3, 20), ("Coffee", 2, 45), ("Juice", 1, 60))\ntotal = 0\nfor product, qty, price in sales:\n    total += qty * price\nprint(total)', "Unpacking three values per row gives clear names for the maths: 60 + 90 + 60 = 210."),
 F(3, IMM, "This code should print `(3, 4, 5)`, but it crashes. Fix it.", 'point = (3, 4)\npoint.append(5)\nprint(point)', 'point = (3, 4)\npoint = point + (5,)\nprint(point)', "Tuples have no `append()`. Join a new one-item tuple with `+`, and remember the comma in `(5,)`."),
 T(3, PACK, 'first, *rest = "data"\nprint(first, rest)', "Unpacking works on any sequence, text too. `first` gets `d` and the starred name gets the rest as a list."),
 W(3, IMM, '`scores = (72, 55, 90, 64)`. Make a sorted tuple (lowest first) and print it. Then print the highest score using an index of that sorted tuple.', 'scores = (72, 55, 90, 64)\n', 'scores = (72, 55, 90, 64)\nranked = tuple(sorted(scores))\nprint(ranked)\nprint(ranked[-1])', "`sorted()` returns a list, so wrap it in `tuple()`. The biggest value is then the last item."),
 W(3, CRE, 'Build a list of the squares of 1 to 5 with a loop, turn it into a tuple, and print the tuple and its type with one `print()`.', 'squares = []\n', 'squares = []\nfor n in range(1, 6):\n    squares.append(n * n)\nresult = tuple(squares)\nprint(result, type(result))', "Collect in a list (it can grow), then lock the finished data in a tuple with `tuple()`."),
